air itself.  \n\nI need to aim for a hand that can reach 8 points. Possible patterns:  \n- All Pungs (6 points) + Dragon Pung (2 points) but I have no dragons.  \n- Half Flush (6 points) + other patterns: Half Flush requires only honor tiles and one suit. My hand currently has three suits, so I would need to discard all tiles from two suits. That's difficult.  \n- Full Flush (24 points) but I have multiple suits.  \n- Seven Pairs (24 points) but I only have one pair currently.  \n- Concealed Hand (2 points) etc.  \n\nGiven the meld is exposed (Chi T5), I cannot have Fully Concealed Hand.  \n\nMaybe I can aim for All Pungs (6 points) plus Prevalent Wind (2 points) plus Pung of Terminals or Honors (1 point for each pung) to reach 8. For example, if I form pungs of F3, F1, B1, and pair of T8, that would be All Pungs (6) + Prevalent Wind (2) + Pung of Terminals or Honors (1 for F3, 1 for F1, 1 for B1) = 11 points. But I need to draw those tiles.  \n\nAlternatively, if I form pungs of F3, T8, and something else, and a pair of something.  \n\nGiven the wall, B1 and B9 have few left, so pungs are unlikely. F1 and F3 have some left. T8 has 2 left.  \n\nSo, I should keep F3, T8, and maybe B5 for flexibility. Discard terminals that are hard to complete: B1 and B9.  \n\nBetween B1 and B9, which is safer? Looking at discards:  \n- My history: I played B1 earlier. Others didn't claim. Next player has melds with B1,B2,B3 and B4,B4,B4, so they have dots. They might need B1 or B9? They already have B1 in a chow, so maybe they don't need another. Previous player has melds with F2 and W7,W8,W9. They might need B9 for something? Not sure.  \n\nDiscard B9 might be safer because B1 is a terminal that could be used for chow by someone with B2,B3? But B2 is exhausted, so no one can chow B1 with B2,B3. So B1 is only useful for pung. Similarly, B9 only for pung or chow with B7,B8.  \n\nUnshown B7:2, B8:3, so B9 chow is possible for others.  \n\nBetter to discard B9 first? But I have B1 also